# 12. Conclusion — Author Statement

TenantSage™ was designed to make authority and governance structural rather than optional.

Its original TenantSage Real-Time Inheritance Retrieval Path™ remains a core technical retrieval mechanism.

The current architecture extends that mechanism into an end-to-end authority, evidence, generation, validation, and execution control plane.

The defining property of TenantSage is not simply that AI can retrieve information or perform actions.

It is that the AI does not control the authority under which those capabilities operate.

The architecture determines:

**Who is making the request?**

**What authority do they possess?**

**What scope is active?**

**What evidence is eligible before semantic retrieval?**

**What governed evidence may enter generation?**

**What proposed action is valid?**

**What exact action has been approved?**

**What may actually execute?**

**Can the resulting outcome be proven?**

The AI can reason.

It can plan.

It can retrieve within an authorized boundary.

It can generate.

It can propose actions.

But it cannot enlarge its own authority.

That boundary is the responsibility of TenantSage.

This document records the intent, structure, technical ancestry, and current canonical choreography of that system.

